I am Surma I am a developer advocate for  the open web platform and work for with  a chrome team for Google in London and I  have the great pleasure today talk about  one of my newly found passions which is  web assembly you have probably heard of  it a bit a little bit and you can  contact me on Twitter if you have any  questions in the future and later on my  colleague deeply from the web  engineering team is gonna talk a bit  about the future of web assembly um  before we start though I wanted to bring  us all onto the same page because web  assembly is often associated very  tightly with c+ nuts so much so that a  lot of people think it is all about C++  when in fact web assembly is so much  more than that many of the demos that  you can find online are about C++ and M  scripting and that makes sense because M  strippin is an absolutely amazing tool  but it is very important for web  developers to realize that it's not just  this worthless and that web assembly by  itself is actually a really useful tool  to have in your back pocket and that's  what I want to talk about in this talk I  want to show some other languages that  support web assembly how you can use web  assembly maybe without learning a new  language and then as I said Deepti it's  going to talk a bit about what the  future of web assembly might hold so to  just make sure everybody knows what  we're here for  this is what assembly dot-org where we  can explain what web assembly is it is a  stack based virtual machine and if you  don't know what a web stack based  virtual machine is that is absolutely  okay what is important though is that  you realize it is a virtual machine  meaning it's a processor that doesn't  really exist but something that has been  designed to easily compile to a lot of  actual real architectural and that it's  called portability so it is a virtual  machine designed to prioritize  portability so when you write some code  in whatever language and you compile it  to web assembl